window.HS=window.HS||{};HS.BOOKSRC=HS.BOOKSRC||{};HS.BOOKSRC["omar_finishing_en"]={"meta":{"title":"Site Finishing & Execution","short":"Site Finishing & Execution","subtitle":"The complete practical guide from site handover to key handover — to Egyptian, Saudi and international codes","by":"Eng. Omar Khamis","edition":"First edition — 2026","year":"2026","footer":"Eng. Omar Khamis — Site Finishing & Execution","editable":true,"codes":["ECP","SBC","IBC / IPC","ASTM","BS EN","TMS 602"],"dedication":"To my parents — may God forgive them and reward them for all they gave me.\nAnd to every site engineer who stands under the sun turning drawings into homes.","coverImg":"finishing.jpg","backImg":"finishing.jpg","blurb":"This book walks through a finishing site item by item, in the real order of work: from the site handover record and setting out, through masonry, rough-ins, plaster, waterproofing, floors, walls, ceilings, joinery, aluminium and painting, to the snag list and the handing over of the keys.\nFor every item: materials and their specifications, step-by-step execution, requirements quoted by clause from the Egyptian and Saudi codes and international standards, common defects and their remedies, ready checklists, and blank and filled inspection forms.\nWritten for site engineers, consultants, contractors and students — and for anyone who wants work accepted right the first time.","about":"Eng. Omar Khamis is an Egyptian architect working in interior design, construction and the supervision of finishing works, and the founder of the Omar Khamis Library for SketchUp, Revit, 3ds Max and AutoCAD used by designers across Egypt and the Arab world.\nHe wrote this book from the site: from the questions asked every day between engineer, contractor and owner, and from the mistakes that cost a great deal when they are found after handover.","lang":"en"},"files":[{"n":"00_front.md","md":"#* Author's Preface\nOn every project there comes a day when the owner stands at the door of a flat or a villa and receives the keys. At that moment the owner sees neither the concrete nor the steel nor the structural calculations. The owner sees the **finishes**: how straight the wall looks under the window light, how true the tile joints run, how smooth the paint is, how well the door closes, and how quietly the cistern refills. Finishes are the face by which everyone who worked on the project is judged.\nYet most of what we know about finishing works on Arab sites has been passed on by word of mouth — from contractor to engineer, from foreman to apprentice. Some of it is sound and proven; some of it is habit that costs millions every year in damp, cracks, hollow-sounding tiles and pipes that leak after handover.\nI wrote this book to be the reference I wished I had on my first day on site: a book that follows the work items **in the real order of execution**, and for each one tells you which materials are right, how the work is done step by step, what exactly the code requires and under which clause, which defects keep repeating and how to catch them before they are buried under plaster or tiles, and which form you use to inspect and sign off the item.\nEvery chapter draws on the **Egyptian codes** and the Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items, on the current **Saudi Building Code (SBC)**, and on the **international standards** behind them — the International Building Code (IBC), the International Plumbing Code (IPC), ASTM standards and the European BS EN standards. Where the codes differ on a value, the values are set side by side in one table so you can see the difference and apply what your contract requires.\n!> Values in this book are given for guidance and are taken from the codes cited, in the editions available at the time of writing. The binding reference is always the code in force where the project is built, the particular specification and the approved contract drawings.\nI pray that this book will be of benefit, and that it will be a lasting charity for me and for my parents.\n**Eng. Omar Khamis**\n\n#* How to Use This Book\nThe chapters follow the **sequence of work on site**. You can read the book from cover to cover as a complete course, or go straight to the item you are working on today.\n## How each chapter is built\nEach chapter opens with a page listing **what you will learn**, and then follows roughly the same order:\n1. **Materials**: types, sizes, standards, and how to accept a material when it arrives on site.\n2. **Before you start**: the conditions that must be met before the item begins (preceding work, approvals, tools).\n3. **Execution**: numbered and illustrated steps.\n4. **Code requirements**: quoted by clause number in dedicated boxes.\n5. **Common defects and their remedies**.\n6. **Quantities**: material consumption rates per unit.\n7. **Inspection**: a ready checklist, a blank inspection form and a filled example.\n## Boxes and symbols\n> **Note**: explanation or useful background.\n!> **Warning**: a common mistake or a risk to safety or quality.\nCODE> **Code reference**: the text of a requirement with the code and clause number, for example: SBC 201 — Section 3306.2.\nTIP> **Site tip**: practical experience that saves time or money.\nEX> **Example**: a worked problem with numbers.\nKEY> **Key point**: what you must take away from the section.\n## Codes and standards\nT: Codes and standards used in this book\n| Abbreviation | Title | Issued by |\n|---|---|---|\n| ECP 203 | Egyptian Code for the Design and Construction of Concrete Structures (2017/2020) | Ministry of Housing — Egypt |\n| ECP Masonry | Egyptian Code for the Design and Execution of Masonry Works | Ministry of Housing — Egypt |\n| EGS | Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items (plaster, painting, marble, joinery, aluminium, insulation, sanitary…) | Housing & Building National Research Center — Egypt |\n| ES | Egyptian Standard Specifications for materials | Egyptian Organization for Standardization and Quality |\n| SBC 201 | Saudi Building Code — General Building Code | Saudi Building Code National Committee |\n| SBC 305 | Saudi Building Code — Masonry Structures | Saudi Building Code National Committee |\n| SBC 701 | Saudi Building Code — Plumbing Code | Saudi Building Code National Committee |\n| SBC 1101 | Saudi Building Code — Residential Building Code | Saudi Building Code National Committee |\n| IBC / IPC | International Building Code / International Plumbing Code | ICC — USA |\n| TMS 602 | Specification for Masonry Structures | The Masonry Society / ACI |\n| BS EN | European standards adopted in the UK | BSI / CEN |\n## Units\nAll dimensions are metric: **mm** for fine dimensions, **cm** for thicknesses and heights, **m** for lengths and levels. Pressure is given in kilopascals (kPa) and bar (1 bar ≈ 100 kPa).\nREF: Egyptian Code for the Design and Construction of Concrete Structures ECP 203-2017, Ministry of Housing, Utilities and Urban Communities, Cairo.\nREF: Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items — Volume 2, Part 4: General, Administrative and Financial Expenses, Housing & Building National Research Center, Cairo.\nREF: Saudi Building Code SBC 201-CR-18, General Building Code, Saudi Building Code National Committee, 2018.\nREF: Saudi Building Code SBC 305-CR-18, Masonry Structures, 2018.\nREF: Saudi Building Code SBC 701-CR-18, Plumbing Code, 2018.\nREF: International Code Council, International Building Code (IBC) and International Plumbing Code (IPC).\nREF: TMS 602 / ACI 530.1 / ASCE 6, Specification for Masonry Structures.\n"},{"n":"01_site.md","md":"# Site Handover and Preparatory Works\n@image: img/omar_finishing/ch01.jpg | The accepted concrete frame: the starting point of every finishing item\n@lead: The first day on site shapes the whole project. A hasty handover of a site, or of a concrete frame full of errors, means that every finishing item that follows pays for those errors: thicker plaster than necessary, floors laid to the wrong falls, doors that will not close. This chapter shows how to take over a site on paper and on the ground, and how to prepare it so that work can start safely and in good order.\n@goals: The documents you must hold before taking over a site | How to survey a site and record its condition with photographs and measurements | Accepting a concrete frame for finishing works against the tolerances of ECP 203 | Setting out, transferring levels and the +1.00 m datum line | Temporary site facilities to the Egyptian General Specifications and the safety requirements of SBC 201 | The correct order of finishing works from handover to keys\n@refs: ECP 203-2017, Chapter 9, Clause 9-9-2 \"Permissible dimensional tolerances\" and Table (9-1) | Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items, Vol. 2, Part 4: General and Administrative Expenses, Item 2/2 \"Temporary construction facilities\" | SBC 201-CR-18, Chapter 33 \"Safeguards during construction\", Sections 3301–3308 | FIDIC Conditions of Contract for Construction (Red Book) 2017, Sub-Clause 8.1 Commencement of Works\n\n## What does \"site handover\" mean?\nOn our sites the single phrase \"taking over the site\" is used for three different events. It matters to tell them apart, because each has its own paperwork and its own responsibilities:\n1. **Handing the site to the contractor** (site possession): the owner or the owner's representative hands the land or building to the contractor so that work can begin. From this date the **time for completion** in the contract usually starts, and the contractor becomes responsible for the site, its security and the safety of everyone on it.\n2. **The finishing contractor accepting the concrete frame** from the structural contractor (or from the owner): the finishing contractor measures the frame and records any errors before starting, so as not to bear later the cost of correcting other people's mistakes.\n3. **The consultant inspecting completed work** item by item during construction — the subject of the following chapters and of the \"inspection request\" forms at the end of each.\nKEY> No work starts on site without a written **record** signed by the parties: the site handover record, the frame acceptance record, and the benchmark and levels record. Spoken agreements protect nobody when a dispute arises.\n[[Site possession]] in contracts based on FIDIC forms is tied to the Commencement Date notified to the contractor in writing, from which the time for completion is counted. In Egyptian contracts it is recorded in a \"site handover record\" signed by the owner, the contractor and the engineer, stating the condition of the site and any existing obstructions.\nGL: تسليم الموقع | Site possession | Giving the contractor access to the site so that execution can begin; the contract period usually runs from it\nGL: تاريخ البدء | Commencement date | The date notified to the contractor in writing to start work, from which the time for completion is counted\n\n## Documents required before handover\nBefore you go to site, assemble a single file (paper and digital) containing the documents below. Any missing document is the first \"remark\" you write in the handover record.\nT: The document file before taking over a site\n| Document | Why you need it | Provided by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contract and annexes (general and particular conditions) | Fixes the start date, duration, penalties and scope | Owner |\n| Building permit and any amendment | Proves the legality of the building, its limits and permitted height | Owner |\n| Approved architectural, structural and MEP drawings — **latest revision** | Work is executed only from the approved revision | Consultant |\n| Particular specification and bill of quantities | Define the quality of every item and how it is measured | Consultant |\n| Soil report (for projects starting from the ground) | Foundation level, groundwater, cement type | Owner / Consultant |\n| Survey drawing and benchmarks (BM) | Every level is transferred from them | Owner / Surveyor |\n| Preliminary programme | Order of work and delivery dates | Contractor |\n| Quality plan and safety plan | Required by most contracts before start | Contractor |\n| List of proposed subcontractors and suppliers | For early approval | Contractor |\n!> Check the **revision number** and date of every drawing. The most expensive mistakes on site come from building from an old drawing that stayed on site after a revision was issued.\nTIP> Stamp every drawing that reaches site with \"Approved for construction — Revision … — Date …\", and keep superseded drawings in a separate file marked \"Superseded — for reference only\". This simple habit prevents half the problems.\n\n## Surveying the site and recording its condition\nA survey is not a quick walk round. It is an organised process whose purpose is to know **everything that affects the work** and to record **the condition of the site before you touch it**.\n### What to check\n- **Boundaries and neighbours**: do the boundaries on the ground match the drawings and permit? Are there encroachments or neighbours' fences inside the plot?\n- **Adjoining buildings**: photograph the party walls and any existing cracks before any excavation or demolition, with the date and a measuring rule next to each crack.\n- **Existing utilities**: power poles, buried cables, water, sewer and gas lines, manholes. Obtain the utility maps from the authorities before digging.\n- **Access**: street width, truck access, permitted storage areas and working hours.\n- **Temporary water and power**: where will they come from, and what capacity is available?\n- **Natural levels**: ground level relative to the pavement and to the approved benchmark.\nCODE> **SBC 201 — Section 3307.1**: Adjoining public and private property shall be protected from damage during construction and demolition, including footings, foundations, party walls and roofs, and provision shall be made to control water runoff and erosion. The person causing an excavation shall give written notice to the owners of adjoining buildings **not less than 10 days** before the scheduled start of the excavation.\n### Photographic record\nThe rule: **a photograph of everything that might later become a matter of dispute**. Photograph every party wall, every crack, every part of the pavement in front of the site, every street light and the condition of the road. Put something in each photograph that identifies the place (plot number or a painted mark) and something that gives the scale (a rule or tape), and file the photographs in order in a folder named \"Site condition before start — date\".\nEX> A contractor started excavating next to an old building without any record. A month later the neighbour claimed that every crack in his building was caused by the excavation and demanded compensation. Dated photographs of the existing cracks taken before the start would have limited the dispute to new cracks only.\n\n## Accepting the concrete frame for finishing works\nWhen a finishing contractor starts from an existing concrete frame, the first duty is to **measure the frame and record its condition**. The aim is not to hunt for errors to bill someone, but to know what will have to be corrected before it turns into an unexpected cost.\n### What is measured\n1. **Plumbness of columns and concrete walls** with a plumb line or laser — two faces of every column.\n2. **Slab levels** at several points in each room (corners and centre) relative to the benchmark.\n3. **Clear heights** from the slab to the soffit of the slab or beam above.\n4. **Opening sizes** (doors, windows, shafts) and their positions relative to the grid.\n5. **Straightness of beams and walls** along their length.\n6. **Concrete surface condition**: honeycombing, exposed reinforcement, cracks, timber or wire left in the concrete.\n7. **Sleeves and openings** left for pipes and their positions compared with the MEP drawings.\n### Tolerances of the Egyptian code\nIn its Chapter 9 the Egyptian concrete code sets maximum deviations of the built dimensions from the design dimensions, and states expressly that these are **the reference where the contract specifies no particular tolerances**. These are the values you compare your measurements with:\nT: Permissible tolerances in a concrete frame — ECP 203-2017 Clause 9-9-2 and Table (9-1)\n| Item | Type of tolerance | Permissible value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grid lines of columns, beams and walls | In any bay or per 6.0 m in any direction | ±5 mm |\n| | Overall dimension of the structure | ±25 mm |\n| Plumb (out of vertical) within one storey | Corner columns and at joints | 3 mm per 3 m height |\n| | Other elements | 5 mm per 3 m height |\n| Plumb over the full height (up to 30 m) | Corner columns and at joints | 15 mm |\n| | Other elements | 25 mm |\n| Offset of a column centre from the storey below | — | The greater of (section width ÷ 40) or 10 mm, provided it is not repeated in two consecutive storeys |\n| Levels of beam and slab soffits | Per 3.0 m horizontally | ±5 mm |\n| | Per bay or per 6.0 m (whichever is greater) | ±10 mm |\n| | Over the full length or width of the structure | ±20 mm |\n| Lintels, sills and cornices on façades | Per bay or 6.0 m | ±5 mm |\n| | Over the full length or width | ±15 mm |\n| Points defining slab or beam levels | Per bay or 6.0 m | ±10 mm |\n| | Over the full length or width | ±20 mm |\n| Openings | Position of opening centrelines | ±15 mm |\n| | Opening sizes | ±5 mm |\n| Sizes of columns and beams, thickness of slabs and walls | Dimensions up to 400 mm | +10 mm or −5 mm |\n| | Dimensions over 400 mm | +15 mm or −10 mm |\nCODE> **ECP 203-2017 — Clause 9-9-2**: \"The tolerances in this clause are the reference to be applied where no particular tolerances are specified in the conditions or drawings of the contract; where any of these tolerances is exceeded, a careful study shall be made to confirm that the design requirements and the provisions of the code are still met.\" The tolerances may not be used to build beyond the property line or to increase permitted projections or heights.\n```fig 640x320\n[[\"r\",40,40,40,260,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"l\",60,20,60,320,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"l\",60,40,72,300,\"\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"dh\",60,72,330,\"Δ\"],[\"t\",150,90,\"plumb line\",12,\"#1d5fa8\",1],[\"t\",165,140,\"column as built\",12,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"dv\",40,300,20,\"3.00 m\"],[\"r\",280,40,40,260,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"l\",300,20,300,320,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"l\",300,40,300,300,\"\",\"#2e7d4f\"],[\"t\",440,150,\"Δ ≤ 3 mm per 3 m (corners)\",12,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"t\",440,180,\"Δ ≤ 5 mm per 3 m (other elements)\",12,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"t\",440,215,\"ECP 203 — Table (9-1)\",11,\"#b07a26\",1]]\n```\n: Checking a column for plumb with a plumb line against the tolerances of the Egyptian code\n### What to do when a tolerance is exceeded\n- **Record it with value and location** in the acceptance record, with a photograph.\n- **Assess its effect on the finishes**: a column 15 mm out of plumb in one storey means 15 mm of extra plaster on one face, or a gypsum lining, or a decision to accept it if the column sits inside a wall that will be clad.\n- **Do not correct it yourself**: any breaking or chiselling of concrete needs the written approval of the structural engineer.\n- **Agree in writing** who bears the cost of the remedy before you start.\n!> Chiselling concrete to correct a lean or hide a projection can expose reinforcement or reduce the concrete cover. Allow it only on the written instruction of the structural designer.\n\n## Setting out and transferring levels\nAfter acceptance, the first technical task in finishing works is **setting out**: transferring the grid and levels from the drawings to the building in a way that every trade can refer to until the last day.\n### The benchmark\nA **benchmark** (BM) is a fixed point of known level from which all levels are transferred. On large sites it is given by the surveyor; in flats and small buildings it is usually agreed as the **finished floor level of the entrance (±0.00)**. What matters is that it is:\n- Fixed, so that it cannot move or be removed during the work (a nail in a concrete column, or a mark on a concrete wall).\n- Recorded in a signed record.\n- Duplicated in more than one place (at least two points) so that one can be restored if the other is damaged.\nGL: نقطة الروبير | Benchmark (BM) | A fixed point of known level from which all levels of the project are transferred\n### The datum line (+1.00 m)\nThe most important tool in finishing works: a horizontal line marked on every wall and column of the floor at **1.00 m above finished floor level**. Every trade measures from it:\n- The plumber: outlet heights (basin, shower, water heater) — for example a basin outlet at 0.55 m is 45 cm **below** the datum line.\n- The electrician: socket and switch heights.\n- The tiler: finished floor level = 1.00 m **below** the line, and the falls from it.\n- The carpenter and aluminium installer: window sills and door heads.\n```fig 640x330\n[[\"r\",0,40,20,260,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",620,40,20,260,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",0,20,640,20,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",20,282,600,18,\"\",\"sand\"],[\"r\",20,276,600,6,\"\",\"tilep\"],[\"l\",20,176,620,176,\"dash\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"t\",320,168,\"Datum line +1.00 (marked with a laser or water level)\",12,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"dv\",276,176,90,\"1.00 m\"],[\"t\",160,268,\"Finished floor level ±0.00\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"dv\",176,236,520,\"0.45\"],[\"r\",500,232,40,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"t\",520,225,\"basin outlet 0.55\",10,\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"dv\",176,146,360,\"0.30\"],[\"r\",350,146,20,14,\"\",\"white\"],[\"t\",360,138,\"socket 1.30\",10,\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"t\",320,315,\"All levels are taken from the datum line, never from the structural slab\",11,\"#6b6152\"]]\n```\n: The datum line: one reference for every trade on the floor\nTIP> Mark the datum line **after** the masonry is finished and immediately before the rough-ins, and ask the engineer to check and sign it at several points. A datum line 2 cm out means every outlet, tile and door will be built wrong by the same amount.\nTIP> The structural slab is not a reference! A slab may vary by ±10 mm or more from one corner to another (see the tolerance table). Always measure from the datum line.\n### Grid lines and wall lines\nColumn grid lines are marked on the slab with a chalk line or laser, and from them the **finished faces of the walls**, the door positions and their openings are drawn. Mark the face of the masonry, not of the plaster, and write the size of each opening on the slab next to it.\n\n## Temporary site facilities\nThe Egyptian General Specifications treat temporary facilities as a separate item of \"general expenses\", defined as the various temporary structures and facilities the contractor provides to meet the contract and execution requirements, including their erection, furnishing, operation and maintenance throughout the works and their removal when no longer needed.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items — General Expenses, Item 2/2 \"Temporary construction facilities\"**: The main facilities are temporary fences, contractor's offices, stores, workshops, temporary housing, internal and access roads, and temporary water, drainage, power and communication networks; they shall be approved by the engineer before construction, without prejudice to safety and public-health requirements.\nT: Basic temporary facilities for a finishing site\n| Facility | Practical requirement | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fence and gate | Encloses the whole site; lockable gate | Height and material per contract or contractor's judgement |\n| Site office | Place for approved drawings, records and site meetings | Displays the programme |\n| Closed store | For sensitive materials: cement, paints, power tools, sanitary ware | Raised, dry floor and ventilation |\n| Open storage yard | Sand, blocks, steel | Away from excavation edges and traffic routes |\n| Temporary water | Tank and pump, or an official connection | Mixing water must be fit for purpose |\n| Temporary power | Distribution board with earth-leakage protection | No exposed cables lying on the ground |\n| Workers' toilets | — | SBC 201 Section 3305.1: sanitary facilities during construction in accordance with SBC 701 |\n| Project signboard | Permit, owner, contractor and consultant details | Often a legal requirement |\n| Fire extinguishers | At stores, distribution board and welding areas | SBC 201 Section 3302.3 refers to SBC 801 |\n```fig 640x400\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,400,\"\",\"clear\"],[\"r\",20,20,600,360,\"\",\"white\"],[\"r\",20,20,600,6,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",20,374,600,6,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",20,20,6,360,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",614,20,6,360,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",260,374,120,6,\"\",\"white\"],[\"t\",320,396,\"Gate\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"r\",160,90,320,200,\"Building\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",40,40,100,60,\"Office\",\"green\"],[\"r\",40,110,100,60,\"Closed store\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",40,190,100,70,\"Toilets\",\"water\"],[\"r\",500,40,100,120,\"Sand and block yard\",\"sand\"],[\"r\",500,180,100,60,\"Water + power\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",500,260,100,60,\"Mixer\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",160,310,320,40,\"Material route\",\"clear\"],[\"c\",600,355,8,\"\",\"red\"],[\"t\",560,340,\"Extinguisher\",10],[\"t\",320,10,\"Street\",12,\"#6b6152\",1]]\n```\n: Example layout of a small finishing site: office, store, storage yards and traffic route kept apart\n\n## Safety during construction\nSafety is not an extra item added when an inspector visits. The Saudi code devotes a whole chapter to it (Chapter 33 of SBC 201) and makes it a condition for continuing work.\nCODE> **SBC 201 — Section 3301.2**: Construction equipment and materials shall be stored and placed so as not to endanger the public, the workers or adjoining property for the duration of the project.\nCODE> **SBC 201 — Section 3302.2**: Waste materials shall be removed in a manner that prevents injury or damage to persons, adjoining properties and public rights-of-way.\n### Protection of pedestrians in front of the site\nWhere a site fronts a street used by the public, the code sets clear requirements:\nT: Pedestrian protection — SBC 201 Section 3306\n| Element | Requirement | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| Walkway | Width not less than **1.2 m**, durable surface, design live load not less than 7.2 kPa | 3306.2 |\n| Construction railing | Height not less than **1.05 m** | 3306.4 |\n| Barrier | Height not less than **2.4 m** on the side of the walkway nearest the work, for the full length of the site; openings protected by normally closed doors | 3306.5 |\n| Covered walkway | Clear height not less than **2.4 m**, adequate lighting at all times | 3306.7 |\n| Excavation near the street line | Excavations 1.5 m or less from the street lot line enclosed by a barrier not less than **1.8 m** high | 3306.9 |\n```fig 640x330\n[[\"r\",0,290,640,20,\"\",\"earth\"],[\"r\",40,60,14,230,\"\",\"wood\"],[\"t\",47,50,\"Barrier ≥ 2.40 m\",12,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"dv\",60,290,30,\"2.40\"],[\"r\",54,170,220,8,\"\",\"wood\"],[\"t\",165,160,\"Covered walkway roof (clear ≥ 2.40 m)\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"r\",270,170,8,120,\"\",\"wood\"],[\"side\",160,290,170],[\"dh\",54,270,305,\"≥ 1.20 m\"],[\"r\",330,226,6,64,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"r\",330,226,90,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"t\",380,215,\"Railing ≥ 1.05 m\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"t\",520,120,\"Work area\",14,\"#6b6152\",1],[\"t\",160,320,\"Pavement / pedestrian walkway\",11,\"#6b6152\",1]]\n```\n: Section through pedestrian protection in front of a site on a public street to SBC 201 Section 3306\n### Personal protective equipment\n- Helmet and safety shoes for everyone entering the site, with no exception for visitors or the owner.\n- Safety glasses for cutting, breaking and grinding; dust masks for sanding and mixing powders.\n- Safety harnesses for work at height or next to unprotected edges.\n- Gloves when handling cement (it causes chemical burns with moisture) and chemicals.\n!> The most serious accidents on finishing sites: falls through shaft and stair openings without guards, electrocution from temporary connections, and falls from improvised scaffolds. Cover or guard every slab opening from the first day.\n\n## The correct order of finishing works\nWhat raises the cost of finishing most is **breaking out after execution**: an electrician breaking new plaster because a conduit was forgotten, or a plumber breaking tiles because the pipes were not tested. The right sequence prevents this. This is the sequence that the chapters of this book follow:\n```fig 660x430\n[[\"r\",20,20,190,46,\"1 Handover and setting out\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",235,20,190,46,\"2 Masonry\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",450,20,190,46,\"3 Rough-ins (plumbing/electrical/HVAC)\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",450,96,190,46,\"4 Tests + rough-in inspection\",\"red\"],[\"r\",235,96,190,46,\"5 Plaster\",\"plaster\"],[\"r\",20,96,190,46,\"6 Waterproofing of wet areas\",\"water\"],[\"r\",20,172,190,46,\"7 Suspended ceilings (frame)\",\"gyps\"],[\"r\",235,172,190,46,\"8 Floors and wall cladding\",\"tilep\"],[\"r\",450,172,190,46,\"9 Door frames and windows\",\"wood\"],[\"r\",450,248,190,46,\"10 Filler and paint preparation\",\"white\"],[\"r\",235,248,190,46,\"11 Joinery and kitchens\",\"wood\"],[\"r\",20,248,190,46,\"12 Fixtures and electrical finishing\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",20,324,190,46,\"13 Final coat of paint\",\"white\"],[\"r\",235,324,190,46,\"14 Cleaning and snag list\",\"green\"],[\"r\",450,324,190,46,\"15 Handover and keys\",\"zone\"],[\"ar\",210,43,235,43],[\"ar\",425,43,450,43],[\"ar\",545,66,545,96],[\"ar\",450,119,425,119],[\"ar\",235,119,210,119],[\"ar\",115,142,115,172],[\"ar\",210,195,235,195],[\"ar\",425,195,450,195],[\"ar\",545,218,545,248],[\"ar\",450,271,425,271],[\"ar\",235,271,210,271],[\"ar\",115,294,115,324],[\"ar\",210,347,235,347],[\"ar\",425,347,450,347]]\n```\n: The general sequence of finishing works from site handover to the keys\nKEY> The golden rule: **everything that will be buried is tested and inspected before it is buried**. Pipes before plaster, waterproofing before tiles, services before ceilings. And everything that soils or breaks easily comes last: the final coat of paint, the sanitary fixtures and the accessories.\nTIP> The order may change in part from project to project: with gypsum ceilings, for example, the frame is fixed and services are run before tiling, while boards and finishes follow to avoid damage. What matters is that the sequence is written into the programme and known to every subcontractor.\n\n## Inspection: checklists and forms\n### Site handover checklist\n- [ ] Contract, permit and approved drawings of the latest revision are in the site office.\n- [ ] At least two benchmarks are set, fixed and signed for.\n- [ ] Boundaries match the drawings and permit, with no encroachments.\n- [ ] Adjoining buildings are recorded with dated photographs, and written notice has been given to neighbours (where there is excavation).\n- [ ] Existing utilities are located and marked on the ground.\n- [ ] The source of temporary water and power is identified.\n- [ ] Locations of office, store, storage yards and traffic route are agreed.\n- [ ] Fence, gate, signboard and fire extinguishers are in place.\n- [ ] Slab and stair openings are covered or guarded.\n### Concrete frame acceptance checklist\n- [ ] Plumbness of columns and walls within ECP 203 Table (9-1).\n- [ ] Slab levels measured at the corners and centre of every room and recorded.\n- [ ] Clear heights match the drawings.\n- [ ] Opening sizes and positions match (±5 mm size, ±15 mm centreline).\n- [ ] No honeycombing, exposed steel or cracks — or, if present, recorded with photographs and an approved remedy.\n- [ ] Sleeves and MEP openings in place.\n- [ ] Formwork remains, wires and nails removed from the concrete.\n\n:::form Site Handover Record\n| Project | ____ | Contract no. | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Owner | ____ | Contractor | ____ |\n| Consultant / Engineer | ____ | Handover date | ____ |\n| Site address | ____ | Plot / unit area | ____ |\nT:\n| Item | Status | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Boundaries match the drawings | [ ] Yes  [ ] No | ____ |\n| Benchmarks (no. / level / location) | ____ | ____ |\n| Existing utilities | ____ | ____ |\n| Obstructions preventing start | [ ] None  [ ] Present | ____ |\n| Documents handed to the contractor | ____ | ____ |\n| Adjoining buildings recorded with ( ____ ) photographs | [ ] Yes | ____ |\nThe date of this record is the date of handing the site to the contractor unless the contract provides otherwise.\n| Owner's signature | ____ | Contractor's signature | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Engineer's signature | ____ | Date | ____ |\n:::\n\n:::filled Site Handover Record\n| Project | Finishing of a residential villa — Fifth Settlement, New Cairo | Contract no. | 2026/014 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Owner | Mr Ahmed Mahmoud | Contractor | Modern Building Contracting Co. |\n| Consultant / Engineer | Eng. Omar Khamis | Handover date | 01/02/2026 |\n| Site address | Plot 112 — Third District | Plot / unit area | 600 m² / built-up area 420 m² |\n| Item | Status | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Boundaries match the drawings | [x] Yes  [ ] No | Match permit no. 345 of 2025 |\n| Benchmarks (no. / level / location) | BM1 = +0.00 entrance floor — BM2 on column C4 = +1.00 | Fixed with a nail and painted red |\n| Existing utilities | Sewer manhole in front of the gate, temporary power meter | Sound |\n| Obstructions preventing start | [x] None  [ ] Present | — |\n| Documents handed to the contractor | 42 architectural, structural and MEP drawings — Revision C | Stamped \"Approved for construction\" |\n| Adjoining buildings recorded with ( 36 ) photographs | [x] Yes | Existing 1.2 m crack in the east neighbour's wall — recorded |\n| Owner's signature | Ahmed Mahmoud | Contractor's signature | Eng. Khaled Saeed |\n| Engineer's signature | Eng. Omar Khamis | Date | 01/02/2026 |\n:::\n\n:::form Concrete Frame Acceptance Record for Finishing Works\n| Project | ____ | Floor / unit | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural contractor | ____ | Finishing contractor | ____ |\nT:\n| Element | Measured value | Permitted (ECP 203) | Complies? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plumb of column ____ (per 3 m) | ____ mm | 3 / 5 mm | [ ] |\n| Slab level — highest point | ____ | ±10 mm per bay | [ ] |\n| Slab level — lowest point | ____ | ±10 mm per bay | [ ] |\n| Clear height | ____ | Per drawings | [ ] |\n| Size of opening ____ | ____ | ±5 mm | [ ] |\n| Centreline of opening ____ | ____ | ±15 mm | [ ] |\n| Surface defects (honeycombing / cracks / exposed steel) | ____ | None | [ ] |\nRemarks and agreed remedy: ____\n| Structural contractor | ____ | Finishing contractor | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Engineer | ____ | Date | ____ |\n:::\n\n:::filled Concrete Frame Acceptance Record for Finishing Works\n| Project | Finishing of a residential villa — Fifth Settlement | Floor / unit | Ground floor |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural contractor | United Construction | Finishing contractor | Modern Building |\n| Element | Measured value | Permitted (ECP 203) | Complies? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plumb of column C4 (per 3 m) | 4 mm | 5 mm | [x] |\n| Plumb of corner column C1 (per 3 m) | 7 mm | 3 mm | [ ] |\n| Slab level — highest point | +3.27 | ±10 mm per bay | [x] |\n| Slab level — lowest point | +3.24 | ±10 mm per bay | [x] |\n| Clear height | 2.93 m | 2.90 m | [x] |\n| Size of main door opening | 1.42 × 2.48 | 1.40 × 2.50 ±5 mm | [ ] |\n| Surface defects | Minor honeycombing under beam B7 | None | [ ] |\nRemarks: Column C1 to be corrected by increasing the plaster on the outer face to 25 mm on expanded metal lath, after approval of the structural engineer. Honeycombing to be repaired with non-shrink repair mortar at the structural contractor's cost. Main door opening to be corrected by the structural contractor before the frame is fixed.\n| Structural contractor | Eng. Yasser Ali | Finishing contractor | Eng. Khaled Saeed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Engineer | Eng. Omar Khamis | Date | 03/02/2026 |\n:::\n\nGL: خط المتر | Datum line (+1.00 m) | A horizontal line one metre above finished floor level from which all finishing levels are measured\nGL: التفاوت المسموح | Tolerance | The maximum acceptable difference between the built and the design dimension\nGL: الرأسية | Plumbness | Verticality of an element, checked with a plumb line or laser\nGL: محضر | Record / Minutes | A written document recording a fact and signed by the parties\nGL: التشوين | Material stockpiling | Storing materials on site in designated areas\n"},{"n":"02_masonry.md","md":"# Masonry: Bricks and Blocks\n@image: img/omar_finishing/ch02.jpg | A straight wall saves half the cost of the plaster\n@lead: Masonry is the first finishing item built on the frame, and everything after it depends on it. A leaning or bulging wall means thicker plaster, higher cost and more cracks; a skewed door opening means a frame that will not fit. This chapter explains masonry units and mortar as defined by the Egyptian code, how a wall is built correctly, and when a wall is rejected.\n@goals: Types of Egyptian masonry units, their sizes and properties under the masonry code and the Egyptian Standards | Mortar types, guide mix proportions and strengths | Building a wall from the first course to the beam soffit as required by the Egyptian code | Lintels, ties to columns and the top joint | Building in hot weather | Quantities of units and mortar per square metre | Inspecting masonry with a checklist and a filled form\n@refs: Egyptian Code for the Design and Execution of Masonry Works — Chapter 2 \"Materials and quality control\" (Tables 2-1, 2-3 and 2-10) and Chapter 9 \"Site studies and execution\" (Clauses 9/3, 9/4, 9/5 and 9/7) | Egyptian Standards ES 1756 and 1524 (clay bricks) and ES 1292 (concrete bricks and blocks) | SBC 305-CR-18 Masonry Structures — definition of running bond and reference to TMS 602 | TMS 602 / ACI 530.1 — Specification for Masonry Structures, Article 3.3 F (Tolerances)\n\n## The wall in a framed building\nIn most of our modern buildings the **concrete frame** (columns, beams and slabs) carries the loads, and the walls are **non-load-bearing**: they divide spaces and protect against weather, noise and heat. But \"non-load-bearing\" does not mean \"unimportant\": the wall carries its own weight, has cupboards, water heaters and televisions hung on it, and moves with the frame as it deflects and expands.\nThe Egyptian masonry code therefore distinguishes between:\n- **Load-bearing walls**, which carry the roof loads to the foundations and have their own design requirements (Chapter 3 of the code).\n- **Non-load-bearing external walls** (curtain walls) and **internal partitions**, built inside the frame after it is complete (Chapters 4 and 5).\nKEY> This book deals with non-load-bearing walls in framed buildings. Any load-bearing wall, or any alteration to an existing wall that carries part of the building, needs a design by a structural engineer.\nGL: حائط غير حامل | Non-load-bearing wall | A wall that carries no structural load other than its own weight, built inside a concrete frame\nGL: القاطوع | Partition | A light internal wall dividing spaces\n\n## Masonry units\nThe Egyptian masonry code lists the permitted units: **fired desert-clay units** (red/clay brick), **concrete units** (normal and lightweight bricks and blocks), **sand-lime bricks**, **gypsum units** and **natural stone**. It requires units to be \"of regular shape with sound faces, arrises and edges, and of uniform colour throughout the section, free of laminations and lumps\".\n### Clay bricks (red brick)\nT: Requirements for fired desert-clay masonry units — Egyptian masonry code Table (2-1)\n| Property | Clay brick (ES 1756 and 1524) | Hollow clay blocks (ES 1756) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sizes (mm) | 250 × 120 × (60, 65, 95, 115 or 130) | 235 × 120 × 250 |\n| Dimensional tolerance (mean of ten units) | ±2 mm | Not stated in the standard |\n| Water absorption (by weight) | Not more than 16% load-bearing, 20% non-load-bearing | Not more than 20% |\n| Efflorescence | Not more than 50% (moderate) | Not more than 50% |\n| Density | 0.9–1.2 g/cm³ perforated load-bearing; not less than 1.6 solid load-bearing | — |\n| Compressive strength — mean of 5 bricks (N/mm²) | Load-bearing: 8 — non-load-bearing solid: 4 — vertically perforated: 4 — horizontally perforated: 3 | 2.5 |\n### Concrete bricks and blocks\nT: Requirements for concrete bricks and blocks — Egyptian masonry code Table (2-3) / ES 1292:1991\n| Property | Bricks | Blocks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sizes (mm) | 250 × 120 × 60 — 250 × 120 × 120 — 250 × 120 × 200 | 400 × (100, 120, 200 or 250) × 200 — 400 × 120 × 250 — 390 × 190 × 190 — 490 × 190 × 190 — 500 × 250 × 210 — 390 × 90 × 190 |\n| Density | Light ≤ 1.4 — medium 1.4–2.0 — heavy > 2.0 g/cm³ | Same classes |\n| Minimum compressive strength (mean / single unit) N/mm² | Load-bearing solid 7.0 / 5.6 — load-bearing hollow 5.0 / 4.0 — non-load-bearing solid 2.5 / 2.0 — non-load-bearing hollow 2.0 / 1.6 | Same values |\n| Face-shell thickness of hollow units | Not less than 20 mm | Not less than 20 mm |\n### Lightweight units\nThe code mentions two kinds of lightweight concrete units: **LECA units** (lightweight expanded clay aggregate), valued for low weight and thermal and acoustic insulation, and **cellular concrete units** (the \"light\" or \"white\" brick of the trade), classified by density and strength. These units need particular care at lintels and fixings because they are locally weaker than solid brick.\n!> Lightweight blocks do not hold fixings and brackets as solid brick does. Plan in advance where wall cupboards, water heaters and televisions will go, and build solid courses or add reinforcement there (see \"Building the wall\").\n### Accepting and storing masonry units\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/3/1 \"Masonry units\"**: Units shall be unloaded by crane or by hand and stacked on a dry, level surface or on pallets in stable rows, and protected from rain and frost by plastic sheeting while allowing air to pass through the stack so that they dry before laying. They shall not be stored directly on soil containing sulphate salts, **nor on newly cast concrete slabs** before these have gained sufficient strength.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/3/2**: Cement and hydrated lime shall be stored dry, away from moisture, on raised platforms, and used **in the order of delivery** to site.\n- [ ] Units are from the approved factory and type, with a test certificate for the consignment.\n- [ ] Sizes are regular (measure 10 random units and take the mean).\n- [ ] No noticeable broken edges or corners, and no cracks.\n- [ ] Clay bricks of even colour without marked white efflorescence, and ringing when struck (a sign of good firing).\n- [ ] Concrete blocks dry and cured before delivery (the code requires them to have dried before delivery to reduce drying shrinkage).\nGL: التزهير | Efflorescence | White salts that appear on the surface of brick or mortar when the water carrying them evaporates\nGL: الليكا | LECA | Lightweight expanded clay aggregate used in light insulating blocks\n\n## Mortar\n### Constituents\nThe code allows ordinary, sulphate-resisting or blended Portland cement in masonry mortar, and **lime is used only mixed with cement**. Sand shall comply with the standard for masonry mortar sand, and water shall be drinkable.\n### Mix proportions and strength\nT: Guide proportions of cement-lime mortars — Egyptian masonry code Table (2-10)\n| Mortar designation | Cement | Hydrated lime | Sand (damp, loose) | Mean compressive strength at 28 days |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 0 – 0.25 | 2.25 to 3.00 times the sum of the cement and lime volumes | 15 N/mm² |\n| 2 | 1 | 0.25 – 0.50 | Same rule | 10 N/mm² |\n| 3 | 1 | 0.50 – 1.25 | Same rule | 5 N/mm² |\n| 4 | 1 | 1.25 – 2.50 | Same rule | 2 N/mm² |\nTIP> On Egyptian sites mortar is usually described as \"1:3 cement mortar\" or by cement content, \"350 kg of cement per m³ of sand\". What matters is that the proportion is written in the specification and that there is a **gauge box** for sand on site, not an \"approximate shovel\".\n### Mixing and use\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/2**: Mortar materials shall be measured accurately; when batching by volume a gauge box of fixed dimensions related to a bag of cement shall be used. Mechanical mixing time is **5–7 minutes** after all constituents are in the mixer; less causes a non-uniform mortar, more reduces bond.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/2 \"Use of mortar\"**: Mortar containing cement shall be used **within two hours** of mixing cement and water; any mortar not used within that time shall be discarded and shall not be re-mixed. In hot weather the working life is shorter, so mortar is prepared in step with use.\n!> \"Reviving\" stiffened mortar with water after it has started to set is the most common bad habit on site. Mortar that has begun to set has lost part of its strength and bond; it belongs in the skip.\n\n## Before laying\n1. **Set out the walls on the slab**: face of wall and door openings with their sizes from the approved architectural drawings (see Chapter 1).\n2. **Clean the bed**: the code requires partition locations to be clean and free of any material so that the mortar bonds to the slab.\n3. **Ties in the columns**: bars or tie strips fixed in the columns (see \"Tying to columns\").\n4. **Damp-proof course** on the ground floor under walls in contact with the ground, with laps between sheets of at least 100 mm.\n5. **Plan services and fixings**: where will vertical pipes run? Where will the water heater and cupboard hang? Build solid courses there.\n6. **Lintels**: precast and ready, or planned to be cast in place.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/8 \"Damp-proof courses\"**: Damp-proof sheets shall be lapped at joints by not less than **100 mm**, shall not be pierced by reinforcement or fixings, and shall not be left uncovered during finishing work.\n\n## Building the wall\n### First course and corners\nThe first course is the foundation of the accuracy of the whole wall. The code calls for \"extreme care with the first course to avoid accumulating errors\".\n1. Spread mortar on the slab and set the first course **level with a straightedge and spirit level**.\n2. At each end of the wall (the corners) lay a unit of the second course, and check both units **with a plumb bob and spirit level**.\n3. Stretch a **line** between the corners along the upper outer arris, and complete the course level to it.\n4. Raise the corners first, then fill between them.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (4) and (6)**: A horizontal line is stretched **for every two courses at most**, a plumb bob is used **every three courses at most** to keep the wall vertical, and a timber straightedge **not less than three metres long** is used in all directions so that no course projects beyond the others — otherwise the plaster will need \"dubbing out\".\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/4 (i)**: When building walls between corners, the line shall be stretched between the corners before laying, and to avoid sagging **the line shall be supported at intervals not exceeding 6 m**.\n### Joints and bond\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clauses 9/4/4 (a) and 9/4/5**: Units are laid on a **full bed** of mortar, and **all vertical and horizontal joints are completely filled**; the thickness of bed and perpend joints is **10–12 mm**.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/4 (i)**: The horizontal distance between perpend joints in successive courses shall be not less than **one quarter of the unit length**, and in no case less than **50 mm for bricks and 75 mm for blocks**.\nThis is the same definition of **running bond** given in the Saudi code SBC 305: perpend joints in successive courses offset horizontally by at least one quarter of the unit length.\n```fig 640x300\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,24,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",20,40,250,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",280,40,250,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",540,40,100,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",0,110,145,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",155,110,250,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",415,110,225,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",20,180,250,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",280,180,250,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",540,180,100,60,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"dh\",145,270,90,\"≥ ¼ length (≥ 50 mm for bricks)\"],[\"l\",275,40,275,240,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"dv\",100,110,600,\"10–12 mm\"],[\"t\",320,270,\"Perpend joints never line up in two successive courses\",12,\"#2b2620\",1]]\n```\n: Running bond: perpend joints offset by at least a quarter of the unit length, joints 10–12 mm thick\n### Perforated and hollow units\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/4 (c) and (g)**: Cellular bricks are laid with their cavities upwards and the cavities filled with mortar; perforated bricks are laid with perforations downwards without filling. In hollow blocks the mortar is spread in **separate strips on the face shells and webs, not more than 30 mm thick**, with the perpend joints completely filled.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (8)**: When building with hollow units, courses of **solid brick** are built at heights shown on the drawings so that electrical or mechanical runs can be fixed or fixings made.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/3 (f)**: When building with lightweight brick, the **jambs of openings** are built in solid clay or solid concrete brick for a length of not less than **250 mm**, as are the bond courses **at mid-height of the wall and below the slab** and the fixing courses.\n### Wetting units\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clauses 9/4/7 and 9/5/1 (3)**: Fired clay bricks **are dipped in water for not more than two minutes** immediately before use, depending on their moisture content. Sand-lime bricks are preferably not wetted, the mortar consistence being adjusted instead. Concrete units **are not wetted**; instead the mortar consistence is adjusted or water-retaining admixtures are used.\nTIP> A dry red brick sucks the water out of the mortar at once, leaving powder with no bond; a brick soaked too long makes the mortar slide and will not stay in place. A quick dip just before laying is right.\n### Lifts\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (5)**: Walls are built in successive lifts according to good practice: **the first lift up to sill level**, **the second up to lintel level**, and then the full height to the soffit of the beams or slab, dividing the height so that courses are equal.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/4 (k)**: When raising corners and advanced work above the general level, **the wall shall not be raised by more than 1.20 m in one stage**; on façades the full storey height shall be completed in one operation.\n```fig 640x380\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,24,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",0,340,640,16,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",20,24,40,316,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",580,24,40,316,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",60,24,520,30,\"Beam\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",60,54,520,286,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",250,120,140,120,\"Window\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",230,100,180,20,\"Lintel\",\"conc\"],[\"l\",60,240,580,240,\"dash\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"l\",60,120,580,120,\"dash\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"t\",120,232,\"Lift 1 to sill\",11,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"t\",120,112,\"Lift 2 to lintel\",11,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"t\",480,70,\"Lift 3 to beam\",11,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"dh\",230,250,96,\"≥150\"],[\"dh\",390,410,96,\"≥150\"],[\"t\",320,370,\"Never raise more than 1.20 m in one stage\",11,\"#2b2620\",1]]\n```\n: Lifts: to the sill, then to the lintel, then to the beam soffit, with a lintel bearing of at least 150 mm each side\n\n## Lintels\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/6**: Masonry above openings shall not bear on door or window frames; lintels shall be provided to carry the load to the walls on each side. **A lintel shall bear on the jambs for not less than 150 mm on each side**, subject to the permissible bearing stress of the unit used, and **shall not bear on broken brick, lightweight brick or foam brick**. No masonry shall be built over a cast-in-place lintel before its concrete has set and hardened, and precast lintels are placed only after curing and drying.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/4/4 (l)**: A lintel shall bear on a **whole masonry unit**, not on part of one.\nEX> A window with a clear opening of 1.20 m. Lintel length = 1.20 + 2 × 0.15 = **1.50 m minimum**. If the wall is of lightweight brick, the jambs of the opening are built in solid brick for at least 250 mm on each side, and the lintel bears on them.\n\n## Tying to columns and the slab\n### Ties to columns\nThe most frequent crack in finishes: **a straight vertical crack where the wall meets the column**. It is caused by the different movement of concrete and masonry, and by the absence of ties between them.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (9)**: In framed buildings walls are tied to the adjoining columns by **steel bars fixed in the columns before casting**, embedded not less than **100 mm** in the column, at vertical intervals of about **500 mm**, and extending not less than **150 mm** into the wall, buried in the bed joints. To align ties with the courses easily, a steel channel may be fixed to the column face in which sliding tie strips can move.\nTIP> Where no starter bars were left in the columns (as is usually the case), use **galvanised tie strips** fixed to the column with mechanical or chemical anchors every 3–4 courses, then lay a **strip of galvanised expanded metal lath** at least 20 cm wide over the joint before plastering (see the plaster chapter).\n```fig 640x320\n[[\"r\",40,20,120,280,\"Column\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",160,20,440,280,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",100,70,200,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"r\",100,140,200,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"r\",100,210,200,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"dh\",100,160,50,\"≥100 in column\"],[\"dh\",160,300,95,\"≥150 in wall\"],[\"dv\",76,146,330,\"≈ 500 mm\"],[\"t\",450,260,\"Tie bars buried in the bed joints\",12,\"#2b2620\",1]]\n```\n: Tying a wall to a column with steel bars buried in the bed joints, per the Egyptian masonry code\n### The top joint under the beam\nA wall shrinks after it is built, and the beam and slab above it deflect over time. If the last course is driven hard under the beam, slab load passes into the wall and it cracks.\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (5)**: Broken brick shall not be used to fill the top gap, **and the top gap is preferably filled with mortar after the wall has completed its shrinkage**.\nTIP> In practice: leave a gap of 2–3 cm under the beam and fill it at least one to two weeks after building, with mortar — or with a flexible material in long walls or under long-span slabs, as instructed by the designer. Do not build the last course of \"tilted bricks\" wedged in.\n!> Newly built walls shall not be loaded before they have gained enough strength, and shall be temporarily braced if exposed to strong wind before they are tied to the slab — **Egyptian masonry code, Clause 9/5/1 (12) and (13)**.\n\n## Curing and protection\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (10)**: Walls shall be **sprayed with water morning and evening for three consecutive days**, taking account of the units used (gypsum walls are not sprayed).\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/5/1 (7)**: Units shall not be knocked after they are set or moved, because this separates the constituents and loses water. A unit disturbed after the mortar has begun to set is removed, cleaned and relaid on fresh mortar.\n### Building in hot weather\nCODE> **Egyptian masonry code — Clause 9/7/1 \"Execution in hot weather\"**: Materials and mortars are kept in the shade, mixers and equipment are sprayed regularly, cool water is used for mixing, and **mortar temperature shall be kept below 50 °C** during construction where **air temperature exceeds 38 °C**. Work is planned to start at the coolest time of day and stops when temperatures rise sharply, and walls are cured at the end of the day by covering them with damp coverings.\nCODE> **SBC 305 — Special inspection requirements**: Preparation, construction and protection of masonry in cold weather (below 4.4 °C) or **hot weather (above 32.2 °C)** are observed in accordance with Article 1.8 of TMS 602.\n\n## Permissible tolerances\nThe Egyptian code requires walls to be checked with a 3 m straightedge and plumbed every three courses. For numerical limits one can refer to the American specification TMS 602 (the reference to which the Saudi code SBC 305 points for construction):\nT: Masonry tolerances to TMS 602 Article 3.3 F (approximate metric conversion)\n| Item | Permitted tolerance |\n|---|---|\n| Bed joint thickness | ±3 mm |\n| Head joint thickness | −6 mm / +10 mm |\n| Plumb | ±6 mm per 3 m, ±10 mm per 6 m, ±13 mm maximum |\n| True to a line | ±6 mm per 3 m, ±10 mm per 6 m, ±13 mm maximum |\n| Level of bed joints | ±6 mm per 3 m, ±13 mm maximum |\n| Location of wall in plan | ±13 mm per 6 m, ±19 mm maximum |\nKEY> For a wall that will be plastered, strict plumb and line save plaster thickness: every 1 cm out of plumb means 1 cm of extra plaster over the whole face of the wall.\n\n## Quantities\n### Units per square metre\nNumber of units = 1 ÷ [(unit length + joint) × (unit height + joint)] — for a wall one unit thick.\nT: Approximate consumption rates (10 mm joints) — before waste\n| Wall | Unit (mm) | Units / m² | Mortar / m² (approx.) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Half-brick (12 cm) | Clay brick 250 × 120 × 60 | 55 | 0.021–0.025 m³ |\n| One brick (25 cm) | Clay brick 250 × 120 × 60 | 110 | 0.050–0.055 m³ |\n| 12 cm | Concrete brick 250 × 120 × 120 | 30 | 0.013 m³ |\n| 20 cm | Block 400 × 200 × 200 | 11.6 | 0.012–0.015 m³ |\n| 10 cm | Block 400 × 100 × 200 | 11.6 | 0.006–0.008 m³ |\nEX> A half-brick red-brick wall 4.0 m long and 2.9 m high with a 0.9 × 2.2 m door. Net area = 4.0 × 2.9 − 0.9 × 2.2 = 11.6 − 1.98 = **9.62 m²**. Bricks = 9.62 × 55 = 529, and with 5% waste ≈ **556 bricks**. Mortar = 9.62 × 0.023 ≈ 0.22 m³, and with 20% waste ≈ **0.27 m³**. At about 350 kg of cement per m³ of sand this needs about 0.27 m³ of sand and 95 kg of cement (about two bags).\n!> These are approximate geometric rates; for pricing rely on your own site experience and the actual unit sizes, since local bricks may differ from the nominal size by several millimetres.\n\n## Common defects and remedies\nT: The most frequent masonry defects\n| Defect | Cause | Remedy / prevention |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vertical crack at the wall–column junction | No ties; different movement | Starter bars or tie strips + expanded lath over the joint before plaster |\n| Horizontal crack at the top of the wall under the beam | Last course wedged; slab deflection | Top gap filled after the wall has shrunk |\n| Diagonal cracks above the corners of openings | Short lintel or lintel on weak units | Lintel bearing ≥ 150 mm on whole units |\n| Bulging or leaning wall | No line, plumb bob or straightedge | Line every two courses, plumb every 3 courses, 3 m straightedge |\n| Empty perpend joints | Haste | Fill all joints; visual check before plaster |\n| White efflorescence on brick | Salts in brick or sand; storage on saline soil | Compliant materials, raised storage, cover the wall from rain |\n| Weak, friable mortar | Re-tempering after set; low cement content | Use within two hours; gauge box |\n| Openings of wrong size | Joinery and aluminium sizes not checked | Opening sizes from the approved door and window schedule |\n\n## Inspection\n### Masonry inspection checklist\n- [ ] Units, cement and sand match the approved samples; test certificates on file.\n- [ ] Wall location and thickness match the drawings.\n- [ ] Joints 10–12 mm and fully filled, vertical and horizontal.\n- [ ] Perpend offset ≥ a quarter of the unit (≥ 50 mm brick, 75 mm block).\n- [ ] Plumb and line: no noticeable deviation under a 3 m straightedge.\n- [ ] Opening sizes and positions match the door and window schedule.\n- [ ] Lintels bear ≥ 150 mm each side on whole units.\n- [ ] Ties to columns installed (bars or strips).\n- [ ] Top gap left or treated as approved.\n- [ ] Solid courses at fixing locations (lightweight/hollow units) built.\n- [ ] Walls cured for three days and cleaned of mortar droppings.\n\n:::form Inspection Request — Masonry\n| Project | ____ | Request no. | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Contractor | ____ | Date | ____ |\n| Location (floor / grid / wall) | ____ | Approved drawing no. | ____ |\n| Units / mortar | ____ | Wall thickness | ____ |\nT:\n| Check | Value / condition | Complies | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Materials and test certificates | ____ | [ ] | ____ |\n| Joint thickness and filling | ____ mm | [ ] | ____ |\n| Perpend offset | ____ mm | [ ] | ____ |\n| Plumb (max. deviation per 3 m) | ____ mm | [ ] | ____ |\n| Line under a 3 m straightedge | ____ mm | [ ] | ____ |\n| Opening sizes | ____ | [ ] | ____ |\n| Lintel bearing | ____ mm | [ ] | ____ |\n| Ties to columns | ____ | [ ] | ____ |\n| Top gap | ____ | [ ] | ____ |\nEngineer's decision: [ ] Accepted   [ ] Accepted with comments   [ ] Rejected\n| Contractor's engineer | ____ | Consultant's engineer | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\n:::filled Inspection Request — Masonry\n| Project | Finishing of a residential villa — Fifth Settlement | Request no. | MS-012 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Contractor | Modern Building Contracting | Date | 18/02/2026 |\n| Location (floor / grid / wall) | First floor — grids B-C / 2-4 — master bedroom and bathroom walls | Approved drawing no. | A-104 Rev.C |\n| Units / mortar | Solid clay brick 250×120×60 — 1:3 mortar | Wall thickness | 12 cm internal / 25 cm external |\n| Check | Value / condition | Complies | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Materials and test certificates | Consignment certificate no. 4471 — strength 5.2 N/mm² | [x] | — |\n| Joint thickness and filling | 10–12 mm | [x] | — |\n| Perpend offset | 125 mm (half brick) | [x] | — |\n| Plumb (max. deviation per 3 m) | 4 mm | [x] | — |\n| Line under a 3 m straightedge | 9 mm at the north wall | [ ] | Bulge in courses 18–22 — to be taken down and rebuilt |\n| Opening sizes | Bathroom door 0.80 × 2.20 | [x] | Matches door schedule |\n| Lintel bearing | 150 mm | [x] | Precast lintel |\n| Ties to columns | Galvanised strips every 4 courses | [x] | — |\n| Top gap | 25 mm left | [x] | To be filled after 14 days |\nEngineer's decision: [ ] Accepted   [x] Accepted with comments   [ ] Rejected — the bulging part of the north wall (courses 18–22 over 2.10 m) to be taken down and rebuilt before plastering and re-inspected.\n| Contractor's engineer | Eng. Khaled Saeed | Consultant's engineer | Eng. Omar Khamis |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\nGL: العرموس | Mortar joint | The layer of mortar between masonry units, horizontal or vertical\nGL: المدماك | Course | One horizontal row of masonry units\nGL: الرباط المتعاقب | Running bond | Perpend joints in successive courses offset by at least a quarter of the unit length\nGL: العتب | Lintel | A horizontal member over an opening carrying the masonry above it\nGL: الحطة | Lift | One stage in raising a wall\nGL: خيط الشاغول | Plumb bob | A weighted line used to check verticality\nGL: القدة | Straightedge | A long straight rule used to check flatness\n"},{"n":"03_plumbing.md","md":"# Plumbing Rough-in: Supply and Drainage\n@image: img/omar_finishing/ch03.jpg | What is buried in the wall must be tested before it is closed\n@lead: Plumbing rough-in is the item the owner never sees, yet it is the first one felt when we get it wrong: a damp patch on the neighbour's ceiling, a sewer smell in the bathroom, or a weak shower. This chapter covers the supply and drainage pipework inside the building: materials, sizes, heights, falls and the clearances the code requires, and then the tests without which no wall is closed.\n@goals: The difference between supply materials (PPR, CPVC and multilayer pipes) and drainage materials (UPVC and PP) and where each is used | Common outlet heights and how they are measured from the datum line | Minimum clearances around fixtures to SBC 701 Section 405.3.1 | Drainage falls, traps and cleanouts to SBC 701 Chapters 7 and 10 | Pressure and leak tests as required by the Egyptian General Specifications and SBC 701 Section 312 — side by side | Inspecting the rough-in with an inspection request and a filled test record\n@refs: Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items — Sanitary works, Item 4/2/4 \"Tests and acceptance — supply pipes\" and Item 4/3/4 \"Drainage and vent pipes\" | SBC 701-CR-18, Sections 312.2 to 312.9 (tests), 405.3.1 (clearances), 424.3 (shower valves), 604.5, 604.8 and 604.9 (supply), 704.1 and 704.2 (falls and sizes), 708.1 (cleanouts), 1002.1 and 1002.4 (traps) | International Plumbing Code (IPC) — the model on which SBC 701 is based | DVS 2207-11: heated-tool socket welding of polypropylene pipes (manufacturers' tables)\n\n## Scope of the plumbing rough-in\nThe **rough-in** stage covers everything run inside walls and floors before plaster and tiles:\n- **The supply network**: pipes from the main valve (or the tank or water heater) to every outlet, hot and cold.\n- **The drainage network**: from every fixture to the stack or the manhole, including traps and floor drains.\n- **The vent network**: the pipes that protect fixture traps from siphonage and terminate above the roof.\n- **Concealed boxes**: concealed cisterns, concealed mixer bodies and floor drain boxes.\nThe fixtures themselves (basins, WCs, exposed mixers) are fitted at a later stage after tiling and have their own chapter.\nKEY> Everything installed at rough-in will be buried under plaster and tiles. **Testing and inspection before closing** are therefore not a formality; they are the only guarantee against breaking out later.\nGL: التأسيس | Rough-in | Running pipes and boxes inside walls and floors before finishing\nGL: القائم | Stack | A vertical drainage or vent pipe serving more than one floor\n\n## Materials\n### Supply pipes\nT: Common supply pipe materials in buildings\n| Material | Use | Jointing | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [[Polypropylene PPR]] | Hot and cold — the most widespread in Egypt and the Gulf | Heat fusion | For hot water use a higher pressure rating (PN20 or PN25) or fibre-reinforced pipe |\n| CPVC | Hot and cold | Solvent cement | Needs curing time before testing per manufacturer |\n| Multilayer (PEX-AL-PEX) | Hot and cold, especially in floors | Press fittings | Flexible, fewer joints |\n| Galvanised steel | Older buildings and some mains | Threaded | The Egyptian specification requires horizontal runs to be laid to a slight fall so they can be fully drained |\n| Copper | Hotels and special projects | Brazed / press | Isolated electrically from steel with dielectric fittings |\nCODE> **Egyptian General Specifications — Sanitary works**: Where pipes of dissimilar metals such as steel and copper are joined, approved special fittings preventing electrical contact between the two metals (dielectric insulation) shall be installed to prevent corrosion.\n### Drainage pipes\nT: Common drainage and vent pipe materials\n| Material | Use | Jointing | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [[UPVC]] | Drainage and vents inside the building, stacks | Solvent or rubber ring | The most widespread; cheap and light |\n| PP (drainage polypropylene) | Internal drainage, hot discharges | Push-fit with seals | Withstands higher temperatures than UPVC |\n| Acoustic PP | Stacks in hotels and premium buildings | Push-fit | Reduces flow noise |\n| HDPE | Below ground and outside the building | Welded | Very robust |\n| Cast iron | Older buildings and where fire resistance is needed | Lead-caulked sockets / modern couplings | The Egyptian specification requires factory hydraulic testing |\n### Accepting materials on site\n- [ ] Material, brand and pressure rating (PN) match the approved sample.\n- [ ] The marking printed on the pipe is legible: material, diameter, wall thickness, standard, production date.\n- [ ] No cracks, deformation or discolouration.\n- [ ] Fittings (elbows, tees, couplers) from the same system and manufacturer.\n- [ ] Stored out of direct sun, on a level surface supporting the full pipe length.\n!> Plastic pipes degrade under prolonged direct sunlight. Do not leave them exposed on the roof for weeks before installation.\n\n## Before you start\nThe plumber does not start the rough-in until these conditions are met, otherwise outlets will move later:\n1. **Masonry is complete** in the bathroom and kitchen, including the walls in which pipes will be buried.\n2. **The datum line is marked and approved** (see Chapter 1).\n3. **Shop drawings for bathrooms and kitchen are approved**: a plan and elevations of every wall showing every fixture and outlet.\n4. **Fixtures are selected**: type of WC (floor-standing or wall-hung, exposed cistern or concealed), type of mixers (exposed or concealed), size of bath or shower enclosure, and water heater. Each fixture has an **installation sheet** with its own heights and dimensions.\n5. **The finished floor level in the bathroom is known**, together with the thickness of the floor build-up (waterproofing, screed and tile), because floor drain outlets are set to it.\nTIP> Ask the owner or designer to select sanitary fixtures **before** the rough-in. A wall-hung WC, for example, needs a concealed cistern with a steel frame and a specific outlet height; if it is decided after the rough-in, the wall will be broken out.\n\n## Outlet heights\nThe heights below are **common site values**, used where no value is given in the fixture's installation sheet or in the shop drawings. The rule is always: **the approved fixture sheet first**.\nT: Common supply and drainage outlet heights above finished floor level\n| Fixture | Supply outlet | Drainage outlet | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wash basin | 55 cm (hot and cold, 15–20 cm apart) | 50 cm in the wall | Basin rim at 80–85 cm |\n| Floor WC with exposed cistern | 20–25 cm beside the WC | In the floor per fixture (usually 18–30 cm from wall to outlet centre) | Outlet diameter 100/110 mm |\n| Wall-hung WC with concealed cistern | Per cistern (usually inside the frame) | Per frame (outlet centre about 23 cm) | Frame fixed before light masonry or inside a thick wall |\n| Bidet spray | 30–40 cm | — | — |\n| Bath | 70–75 cm (mixer) | In the floor under the waste | Leave an access panel to the trap |\n| Shower (mixer) | 100–110 cm | Floor drain | Fixed shower head at 200–210 cm |\n| Electric water heater | 150–180 cm (inlet and outlet) | — | Valve on the cold inlet |\n| Kitchen sink | 50–55 cm | 45–50 cm in the wall | — |\n| Washing machine | 80–100 cm (valve) | 70–80 cm (standpipe with trap) | — |\n```fig 660x380\n[[\"r\",0,0,20,330,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",640,0,20,330,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",20,330,620,20,\"\",\"tilep\"],[\"l\",20,230,640,230,\"dash\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"t\",585,222,\"Datum +1.00\",11,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"r\",60,250,120,30,\"Basin\",\"white\"],[\"c\",105,275,4,\"\",\"water\"],[\"c\",135,275,4,\"\",\"red\"],[\"dv\",275,330,40,\"0.55\"],[\"r\",110,280,20,30,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"dv\",280,330,195,\"0.50\"],[\"r\",230,240,70,90,\"WC\",\"white\"],[\"c\",215,305,4,\"\",\"water\"],[\"dv\",305,330,215,\"0.25\"],[\"r\",340,270,40,8,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"c\",360,225,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"dv\",225,330,330,\"1.05\"],[\"l\",360,225,360,95,\"\",\"#7f8a96\"],[\"c\",360,95,10,\"\",\"glass\"],[\"dv\",95,330,395,\"2.05\"],[\"t\",360,80,\"Shower head\",10],[\"r\",470,120,90,130,\"Heater\",\"white\"],[\"dv\",250,330,580,\"≥1.50\"],[\"t\",330,365,\"Common values — the fixture sheet and shop drawings govern\",11,\"#6b6152\"]]\n```\n: Elevation of a bathroom wall showing common outlet heights above finished floor level\nEX> The datum line in the bathroom is at +1.00. A basin outlet is required at 0.55 m above finished floor, so the plumber measures **45 cm down** from the datum line. Had he measured 55 cm straight from the structural slab, with the slab 12 cm below finished level (waterproofing + screed + tile), the finished outlet would sit at only 43 cm — visibly below the rim of a wall-hung basin.\n\n## Clearances around fixtures\nThe Saudi code (taken from the International Plumbing Code) sets minimum clearances around fixtures, and the plumber is the first to fix them on site, because the outlet centrelines decide the final position of each fixture.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 405.3.1**: A water closet, urinal, lavatory or bidet shall not be set closer than **375 mm** from its **centre** to any side wall, partition, vanity or other obstruction, or closer than **750 mm** centre to centre between adjacent fixtures. There shall be not less than **525 mm** clearance in front of the water closet, urinal, lavatory or bidet to any wall, fixture or door. Water closet compartments shall be not less than **750 mm** wide and **1500 mm** deep for floor-mounted WCs, and **1400 mm** deep for wall-hung WCs.\n```fig 640x360\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,14,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",0,0,14,340,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",180,14,8,326,\"\",\"conc\"],[\"r\",60,14,70,36,\"\",\"white\"],[\"c\",95,85,32,\"WC\",\"white\"],[\"l\",95,14,95,340,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"dh\",14,95,180,\"375 ≥\"],[\"r\",230,14,110,50,\"\",\"white\"],[\"c\",285,45,18,\"Basin\",\"white\"],[\"l\",285,14,285,340,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"dh\",95,285,262,\"≥ 750\"],[\"dv\",64,190,345,\"≥ 525\"],[\"r\",230,190,110,6,\"\",\"clear\"],[\"t\",285,210,\"front clearance\",10,\"#6b6152\"],[\"dv\",14,340,160,\"≥ 1500 (compartment)\"],[\"dh\",14,180,325,\"≥ 750\"],[\"t\",470,120,\"SBC 701 — 405.3.1\",13,\"#b07a26\",1],[\"t\",470,150,\"Clearances are measured from the fixture centre\",11,\"#2b2620\"],[\"t\",470,175,\"to the finished face of the wall (after tiling)\",11,\"#2b2620\"]]\n```\n: Minimum clearances around a WC and basin and the dimensions of a WC compartment to SBC 701 Section 405.3.1\n!> The 375 mm clearance is measured to the **finished face** of the wall after plaster and ceramic, not to the masonry. If the cladding is 3 cm thick, set the outlet centreline at least 405 mm from the masonry face.\n\n## The drainage network\n### Falls\nHorizontal drainage pipes work by gravity, and the fall is what keeps the velocity high enough to carry solids without deposition. A very steep fall is not always better either, because the water runs ahead and leaves the solids behind.\nT: Minimum fall of horizontal drainage pipes — SBC 701 Table 704.1\n| Pipe size | Minimum slope | In practice |\n|---|---|---|\n| 65 mm or less | 2% | 2 cm per metre |\n| 80 to 150 mm | 1% | 1 cm per metre |\n| 200 mm or larger | 0.5% | 5 mm per metre |\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 704.2**: The size of drainage piping shall not be reduced in the direction of flow. A 100 mm by 75 mm water closet connection is not considered a reduction in size.\nEX> A 50 mm shower branch running 3.2 m to the stack. Minimum slope 2%, so the level difference = 3.2 × 0.02 = **6.4 cm**. If the floor build-up available below the tile (waterproofing + fill + screed) is only 12 cm and the outside diameter of the pipe is 5 cm, the branch needs at least 5 + 6.4 = 11.4 cm at the stack end — barely acceptable. This calculation must be done **before** the slab is cast or the floor level fixed.\n### Traps\nEvery fixture needs a **trap** that holds a column of water to stop sewer gases entering the room.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 1002.4**: Each fixture trap shall have a liquid seal of not less than **50 mm** and not more than **100 mm**.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 1002.1**: Each plumbing fixture shall be separately trapped; the vertical distance from the fixture outlet to the trap weir shall not exceed **600 mm**, and the horizontal distance from the centreline of the fixture outlet to the centreline of the trap inlet shall not exceed **750 mm**. A fixture shall not be double-trapped.\n```fig 640x300\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,40,\"\",\"tilep\"],[\"pl\",60,40,60,150,90,180,130,180,160,150,160,110,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":8}],[\"pl\",160,110,560,150,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":8}],[\"r\",0,40,640,6,\"\",\"membrane\"],[\"l\",60,150,160,150,\"dash\",\"#2f6690\"],[\"r\",64,150,92,25,\"\",\"water\"],[\"dv\",110,150,40,\"50–100\"],[\"t\",210,200,\"trap seal\",12,\"#2f6690\",1],[\"ar\",300,90,250,118,\"fall ≥ 2% (≤ 65 mm)\"],[\"t\",500,190,\"to the stack\",12,\"#6b6152\"],[\"t\",60,25,\"floor drain / fixture outlet\",11]]\n```\n: The trap, its water seal and the fall of the horizontal branch to the stack\nGL: السيفون | Trap | A bend that holds a water seal to stop sewer gases returning to the room\nGL: الحاجز المائي | Trap seal | The depth of water held in a trap (50–100 mm)\n### Cleanouts\nA pipe that cannot be rodded will be broken out one day. The code therefore requires cleanouts at specific locations, and requires them to be **accessible**, not buried.\nT: Cleanout locations — SBC 701 Section 708.1\n| Location | Requirement | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| Horizontal drainage pipes in buildings | At intervals of not more than **30 m** | 708.1.1 |\n| Change of horizontal direction greater than 45° | A cleanout at the change (one cleanout serves all changes within 12 m) | 708.1.4 |\n| Junction of building drain and building sewer | At the junction or within **3000 mm** upstream | 708.1.3 |\n| Clearance in front of the cleanout | Not less than **500 mm** for pipes up to 150 mm, **900 mm** for larger | 708.1.9 |\n| Concealment | Required cleanouts shall not be installed in concealed locations (inside walls or closed ceilings) | 708.1.10 |\nTIP> In traditional Egyptian bathrooms a floor gully box collects the basin, shower and bath wastes before the stack. Place its cover where it can be opened without breaking tiles, and never under the bath or a timber vanity.\n\n## The supply network\n### Pressure and water hammer\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 604.8**: Where the static water pressure within a building exceeds **550 kPa** (about 5.5 bar), an approved pressure-reducing valve with strainer shall be installed to reduce the pressure in the distribution piping to not more than 550 kPa.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 604.9**: Flow velocity shall be controlled to reduce the possibility of water hammer, and a **water-hammer arrestor** shall be installed where quick-closing valves are used.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 604.5**: The fixture supply pipe shall terminate not more than **800 mm** from the point of connection to the fixture.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 424.3**: Individual shower and tub-shower valves shall be pressure-balancing, thermostatic or combination valves, with a means of limiting the maximum setting to **50 °C**.\n### Fusion welding PPR pipes\n1. Cut the pipe square and clean with a proper cutter, and clean the pipe end and the fitting of dust and grease.\n2. Mark the insertion depth on the pipe according to the diameter.\n3. Heat pipe and fitting together on the welding machine at about **260 °C** for the time in the manufacturer's table.\n4. Push the pipe straight into the fitting **without twisting**, and hold them aligned during the working time.\n5. Let them cool without load before testing.\nT: Guide welding times for PPR pipes (manufacturers' tables based on DVS 2207-11)\n| Outside diameter | Insertion depth (mm) | Heating time (s) | Working time (s) | Cooling time (min) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 20 mm | 14 | 5 | 4 | 3 |\n| 25 mm | 15 | 7 | 4 | 3 |\n| 32 mm | 16.5 | 8 | 6 | 4 |\n| 40 mm | 18 | 12 | 6 | 4 |\n| 50 mm | 20 | 18 | 6 | 5 |\n| 63 mm | 24 | 24 | 8 | 6 |\n!> The table is a guide; **the table of the manufacturer of the pipes in use is binding**. Overheating narrows the bore with a ring of molten material, while underheating gives a joint that looks sound and separates under pressure.\n### Practical rules for routing\n- Run pipes in walls **vertically and horizontally** only, never diagonally, so every later trade knows where they are.\n- Do not run supply pipes in the floor under tiles except insulated and in a sleeve that allows expansion, with as few joints as possible.\n- Insulate hot water pipes and keep them apart from cold.\n- Give every bathroom its own isolating valve in an accessible place.\n- Cover in the wall: the mortar should cover the pipe by an adequate thickness (at least 2 cm as a rule of thumb) to avoid cracks above it.\n!> Chases for pipes in walls shall be cut with a chasing saw as two parallel cuts and then cleared — **not hacked out with a hammer** — and kept as shallow as possible. Long horizontal chases in load-bearing walls need the approval of the structural engineer.\n```fig 640x300\n[[\"r\",0,0,640,260,\"\",\"brick\"],[\"r\",0,260,640,40,\"\",\"tilep\"],[\"pl\",120,260,120,150,520,150,520,260,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":6}],[\"pl\",150,260,150,175,490,175,490,260,{\"s\":\"#b3402f\",\"w\":6}],[\"c\",120,150,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"c\",520,150,6,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"t\",320,140,\"Cold\",12,\"#1d5fa8\",1],[\"t\",320,198,\"Hot (insulated)\",12,\"#b3402f\",1],[\"r\",300,60,40,40,\"Valve\",\"white\"],[\"t\",320,240,\"Vertical and horizontal runs only\",12,\"#2b2620\",1]]\n```\n: Supply pipes run vertically and horizontally, hot kept apart from cold, with a separate valve for the bathroom\n\n## Tests before closing\nHere the Egyptian specification and the Saudi code agree on most values; the main difference is that the Egyptian specification requires the supply to be tested **twice** and for longer.\n### Supply pipe test\nCODE> **Egyptian General Specifications — Sanitary works, Item 4/2/4/1**: Supply pipes are tested **twice**: first after the pipes are installed and before the fixtures, by plugging all outlets, filling slowly with water while releasing air, then raising the pressure to **8.8 kg/cm² (125 psi)** or one and a half times the working pressure, whichever is greater, for **3 hours** without leakage or pressure drop; and second after the fixtures, mixers and valves are installed, at **5.2 kg/cm² (75 psi)** or one and a half times the working pressure, whichever is greater, for **3 hours**.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 312.5**: Upon completion of a section or the entire water supply system, it shall be tested and proved tight under a water pressure not less than the **working pressure**, or — for piping other than plastic — by an air test of not less than **344 kPa**, held for not less than **15 minutes**, with water from a potable source.\n### Drainage and vent test\nCODE> **Egyptian General Specifications — Item 4/3/4/1**: Drainage and vent pipes are tested before fixtures are connected, either by a **water test**: plugging all openings except the highest and filling with water so that the test pressure is not less than a **3.00 m** water column, held at least **15 minutes** before the joints are inspected; or by an **air test** at a steady **0.344 kg/cm² (5 psi)**, or the equivalent of a **25.4 cm (10 in)** mercury column, with no drop for **15 minutes**.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Sections 312.2 and 312.3**: Water test with a head of not less than **3000 mm** held at least 15 minutes; air test at **34.5 kPa** or the equivalent of 250 mm of mercury for 15 minutes. The Saudi code expressly states that **plastic piping shall not be tested using air**.\n### Final test after the fixtures\nCODE> **Egyptian General Specifications — Items 4/3/4/2 and 4/3/4/3**: After the fixtures are installed and their traps filled, the system is tested **with air** (a U-gauge connected to a WC trap on the top floor raised to **38 mm** of water, with no drop for **3 minutes**) or **with smoke** (pressure equivalent to a **2.54 cm** water column). Then the **performance test**: fixtures are filled to overflow level and their plugs pulled at the same time, and no trap siphonage or overflow shall occur.\nCODE> **SBC 701 — Section 312.4**: The final test is a detailed visual inspection; where a smoke test is used, a pressure equivalent to **248.8 Pa** (one inch of water) is held for not less than **15 minutes**.\nT: Plumbing rough-in tests — Egyptian General Specifications vs SBC 701\n| Test | Egyptian General Specifications | SBC 701 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Supply — before fixtures | 8.8 kg/cm² (125 psi) or 1.5 × working — 3 hours | ≥ working pressure — 15 minutes |\n| Supply — after fixtures | 5.2 kg/cm² (75 psi) or 1.5 × working — 3 hours | — |\n| Supply by air | — | 344 kPa — non-plastic pipe only |\n| Drainage — water | 3.00 m head — 15 minutes | 3000 mm head — 15 minutes |\n| Drainage — air | 0.344 kg/cm² (≈ 34 kPa) — 15 minutes | 34.5 kPa — 15 minutes; not permitted for plastic |\n| Final — air | 38 mm water — 3 minutes | — |\n| Final — smoke | 2.54 cm water | 248.8 Pa (≈ 2.54 cm water) — 15 minutes |\n| Fixture performance | Fill to overflow and release together without trap siphonage | Visual inspection |\n| Shower floor liner | — (covered in the waterproofing chapter) | 50 mm of water at the threshold — 15 minutes (312.9) |\nKEY> Write the stricter requirement into the contract: the Egyptian pressure (125 psi or 1.5 times) and duration (3 hours) for the supply test, and no air testing of plastic drainage as the Saudi code requires.\n```fig 640x300\n[[\"pl\",40,200,560,200,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":6}],[\"pl\",120,200,120,120,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":5}],[\"pl\",300,200,300,120,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":5}],[\"pl\",480,200,480,120,{\"s\":\"#1d5fa8\",\"w\":5}],[\"r\",112,108,16,12,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",292,108,16,12,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",472,108,16,12,\"\",\"dark\"],[\"t\",300,95,\"Plugs on every outlet\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"c\",600,200,26,\"\",\"white\"],[\"l\",600,200,612,186,\"thick\",\"#b3402f\"],[\"t\",600,245,\"Pressure gauge\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"r\",20,190,20,20,\"\",\"steel\"],[\"t\",30,240,\"Test pump\",11,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"t\",330,270,\"8.8 kg/cm² or 1.5 × working — 3 hours with no drop\",12,\"#b07a26\",1]]\n```\n: Arrangement of the hydraulic pressure test of supply pipes before the fixtures are fitted\n\n## Common defects and remedies\nT: The most frequent plumbing rough-in defects\n| Defect | Result | Prevention / remedy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Heights measured from the structural slab | Outlets too low or too high after tiling | Always measure from the datum line |\n| Insufficient or reverse fall | Deposits, blockage and smells | Check the fall with a level or laser before backfilling and record it |\n| Size reduced in the direction of flow | Repeated blockage | Prohibited by SBC 701 704.2 |\n| Two traps on one fixture | Gurgling and slow drainage | One trap per fixture (1002.1) |\n| PPR overheated during welding | Narrowed bore and weak flow | Follow the manufacturer's table; temperature-controlled machine |\n| Supply not tested before plaster | Leak after finishing and breaking out | No plaster before a signed test record |\n| Cleanout buried under tiles | Breaking out at the first blockage | Accessible cleanouts (708.1.10) |\n| Floor outlet off the centre of the selected WC | Offset connectors or breaking out | Select the WC before the rough-in |\n| Dissimilar metals joined without isolation | Corrosion and rust at the joints | Dielectric fittings |\n\n## Inspection\n### Plumbing rough-in checklist\n- [ ] Materials match the approved samples (type, brand, pressure rating).\n- [ ] Every outlet matches the approved shop drawings: centreline and height from the datum line.\n- [ ] Clearances around fixtures achieved to the finished face (375 / 750 / 525 mm).\n- [ ] Drainage falls measured and recorded (2% up to 65 mm, 1% up to 150 mm).\n- [ ] No reduction of size in the direction of flow.\n- [ ] Traps and floor drains in place with a 50–100 mm seal.\n- [ ] Cleanouts in place and accessible.\n- [ ] Hot pipes insulated and separated from cold; separate valve for each bathroom.\n- [ ] Signed supply pressure test record (pressure, duration, readings).\n- [ ] Signed drainage test record.\n- [ ] Outlets closed with protective plugs and colour-coded (blue cold, red hot).\n- [ ] Photographs of every wall before plaster kept in the project file (to locate pipes later).\nTIP> Photograph every wall with pipes before it is plastered, with a tape measure stretched beside the pipes. These photographs will save you hours when someone wants to hang a mirror or a cupboard years later.\n\n:::form Inspection Request — Plumbing Rough-in\n| Project | ____ | Request no. | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Contractor | ____ | Date and time requested | ____ |\n| Location (floor / unit / bathroom) | ____ | Approved shop drawing no. | ____ |\n| Item to be inspected | [ ] Supply  [ ] Drainage  [ ] Vent  [ ] Concealed boxes | | |\nT:\n| Check | Complies | Does not comply | Remarks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Materials match the approved sample | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Outlet positions and heights | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Clearances around fixtures | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Drainage falls | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Traps and cleanouts | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Hot pipe insulation and valves | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\n| Pressure test (record no. ____ attached) | [ ] | [ ] | ____ |\nEngineer's decision: [ ] Accepted   [ ] Accepted with comments   [ ] Rejected — resubmit after correction\n| Contractor's engineer | ____ | Consultant's engineer | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\n:::filled Supply Pipe Pressure Test Record\n| Project | Finishing of a residential villa — Fifth Settlement | Record no. | PL-T-07 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Location | First floor — master bathroom and guest bathroom | Pipe type | PPR — PN20 / PN25 for hot |\n| Expected working pressure | 3.5 bar | Required test pressure | 8.8 kg/cm² (greater than 1.5 × 3.5) |\n| Type of test | [x] First, before fixtures  [ ] Second, after fixtures | Reference | EGS Sanitary works 4/2/4/1 |\n| Time | Gauge reading | Remarks | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 09:00 | 8.8 kg/cm² | Test started after air released | |\n| 10:00 | 8.8 kg/cm² | No visible leak | |\n| 11:00 | 8.7 kg/cm² | Ambient temperature rise — joints checked: sound | |\n| 12:00 | 8.7 kg/cm² | Test completed | |\nResult: [x] Accepted   [ ] Rejected — the 0.1 kg/cm² drop is due to temperature change with no visible leak found on any joint.\n| Contractor's engineer | Eng. Khaled Saeed | Consultant's engineer | Eng. Omar Khamis |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\nEX> Note that in the record above the engineer accepted a small drop (0.1 kg/cm²) after checking every joint and relating it to temperature change. This is an engineering decision whose reason must be written explicitly in the record. If the drop recurs or grows, the test is repeated after the leak is found.\n\nGL: صمام تخفيض الضغط | Pressure-reducing valve (PRV) | A valve that reduces network pressure to a set value\nGL: مانع الطرق المائي | Water-hammer arrestor | A device that absorbs the pressure surge when valves close quickly\nGL: طبة تسليك | Cleanout | An opening with a cover that allows a drainage pipe to be rodded\nGL: اختبار الدخان | Smoke test | Pumping dense smoke into a drainage system to find leaks\nGL: بطانة أرضية الدش | Shower liner | A waterproof layer under a shower floor turned up the walls at least 50 mm\n"}]};
